  "skillId": "evaluate-simplify-logs",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "To evaluate a logarithm to a number, rewrite the number inside the log as a power of the base — then the logarithm is simply that index. Reciprocals give negative indices, roots give fractional indices, and a decimal that is a power of $10$ has an integer index. When several logs are combined, first merge them with the log laws until a single log of a power of the base remains, then read off the index.",
    "facts": [
      "The key move is $\\log_{a}a^{k}=k$: once the argument is written as a power of the base, the value is that index.",
      "A reciprocal gives a negative index: $\\log_{2}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\log_{2}2^{-3}=-3$.",
      "A root gives a fractional index: $\\log_{2}\\sqrt{8}=\\log_{2}2^{\\frac{3}{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}$.",
      "Powers of ten cover decimals: $\\log_{10}1000=3$ and $\\log_{10}0.001=-3$.",
      "For a combination, use the product, quotient and power laws to reach a single logarithm first, then evaluate it."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "If the expression is a combination of logs, use the log laws to merge it into a single logarithm.",
      "Write the number inside the logarithm as a power of the base.",
      "The value of the logarithm is that index."
    ]
  },
